 "title": "Fractional exponents and radicals",
 "audience": "learner",
 "keyIdea": "a^(1/n) is the nth root of a, and a^(m/n) is that root raised to the power m.",
 "explain": {
  "kid": [
   "9 = 3 × 3. So 3 is the square root of 9. We can also write it as 9 to the power 1/2.",
   "27 = 3 × 3 × 3. So the cube root of 27 is 3, and 27 to the power 1/3 is 3."
  ],
  "adult": [
   "Use the power-of-a-power law: (a^(1/n))ⁿ = a¹ = a. So a^(1/n) is the number that gives a when it is multiplied n times: the nth root.",
   "For a^(m/n), take the root first, then the power: 8^(2/3) = (∛8)² = 2² = 4. Taking the root first keeps the numbers small."
  ]
 },
